{
  "info": {
    "name": "Food Forecast API",
    "description": "# Food Forecast API\n\nPredicts how many portions of each menu item a restaurant will sell, as a **low / likely / high** range.\nAll numbers come from **LightGBM**. The LLM (Groq) only rewords the forecast into a chef message.\n\n## Before you start\n1. Start the server in PowerShell and keep the window open, either:\n   - in `C:\\projects\\food-predection`: `pnpm dev` (starts the API and the Next.js web app), or\n   - in `C:\\projects\\food-predection\\apps\\api`: `python -m uvicorn forecast.api:app --reload` (API only)\n2. Collection **Variables** tab:\n   - `baseUrl` = `http://127.0.0.1:8000`\n   - `restaurantId` = `hyd_demo`. Change it to start a fresh test; each id keeps its own data and models.\n   - `cutoff` = `2026-11-06`. The model trains only on sales up to this day; the uploaded days after it are\n     the hidden test. Change it to test another week.\n3. **Upload files** must be **.csv** or **.json** (anything else is rejected). The requests already point to\n   test data of an imaginary Hyderabad biryani restaurant in `test_data\\hyderabad_demo\\` (2 years, 10 dishes;\n   its README lists the patterns hidden in it). To use your own data, select your file instead. If Postman says a file is missing, open\n   *Body > form-data > file* and select it again, or turn on *Settings > General > Allow reading files\n   outside working directory*.\n\n## Test with your own data (hold-out test)\nRun the folders in order, or the whole collection with the **Runner**:\n1. **1. Upload data** > *Upload sales*: **all** your real sales, once.\n2. **2. Train and forecast** > *Train* (about 40 s), then *Forecast next 7 days*. Both use `until={{cutoff}}`,\n   so the model never sees the days after the cutoff.\n3. **3. Check accuracy** > *Compare per day* / *Compare per week*: the forecast vs your uploaded real sales\n   after the cutoff. No file needed.\n4. To test another week, change `cutoff` and run folder 2 and 3 again.\n\n*Compare with a reference file* is the other way: send real sales in a file that is **not saved**.\n\n**Fairness check:** if the live model was trained on days after the cutoff, Forecast refuses with a clear\nmessage, because it would be testing on days it already saw. Train with the same cutoff first.\n\n## Nightly use (real life, no cutoff)\n**4. Nightly use**: upload the day's sales, *Forecast tomorrow only*, then *Chef message*.\n\n## What the accuracy numbers mean\n- **Accuracy = 100% - (portions missed / portions sold)**, measured on days the model never saw.\n- **Per day:** `accuracy_per_item_per_day`, `accuracy_daily_total`, and `actual_inside_range_percent`\n  (how often real sales fell between low and high; target about 80%).\n- **Per week:** `accuracy_per_item` and `accuracy_restaurant_total` over the forecast days added up. Weekly is\n  higher than daily, because daily ups and downs cancel out.\n- On a date with real sales, an item that is missing counts as **0 sold** (POS exports skip such items).\n\n## File formats (.csv or .json)\n- **Sales CSV:** `date,item,qty_sold,sold_out`. `date` is YYYY-MM-DD only, `qty_sold` is a whole number,\n  `sold_out` (yes/no) is optional.\n- **Sales JSON:** a list of rows:\n  `[{\"date\": \"2026-11-14\", \"item\": \"Chicken Biryani\", \"qty_sold\": 182, \"sold_out\": \"no\"}]`\n  (example: `postman\\sample_files\\sales_example.json`)\n- **Day info:** `date,closed,bookings,promo`, as CSV or the same JSON list style. Future dates are allowed.\n\n## Errors\n- **400:** bad file or value. The message says what is wrong and on which file line.\n- **404:** this restaurant has no sales or no forecast yet.\n- **422:** a parameter has a wrong value (e.g. `period=month`, or a date not in YYYY-MM-DD).\n\nNo login yet: keep the server on your own computer (127.0.0.1) until authentication is added.\n",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
